IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cpp-core`

-- Canada Pension Plan Regulations (C.R.C., c. 385), PART VIII, regulation 85 -- YEAR'S MAXIMUM PENSIONABLE
-- EARNINGS: the time and manner of calculation. Piece cr1. LAYER 1.
--
-- Section 18 of the Act (piece cp1) says what the Year's Maximum Pensionable Earnings is: a ratio of averages of
-- the Wage Measure, a product, and so on. This regulation says when it is calculated and how the averages, ratios
-- and products are rounded. The Wage Measure and the Industrial Aggregate in Canada are Statistics Canada's
-- published series (not deposited): the monthly figures arrive as a LIST OF NUMBER.
-- The text's adjusting rule is stated twice, to two digits (for an average or a product) and to three digits (for
-- a ratio). "Expressed as a decimal fraction of three digits" is read as taking the first three digits after the
-- decimal point, and the third digit then decides: dropped if under five, otherwise the second digit goes up by
-- one (fork F17). That is not the same as rounding to three digits first: 1.2345 gives 1.23, not 1.24.

§ `Regulations, Part VIII -- regulation 85: the Year's Maximum Pensionable Earnings`

§§ `Regulation 85(1): when it is calculated`

-- 85 (1) The Year's Maximum Pensionable Earnings for a year shall be calculated immediately following the date on
-- which the first revision of the Industrial Aggregate in Canada for the month of June of the year preceding that
-- year is published by Statistics Canada. "Following the date" is read as from the next day (fork F18).
-- src:845 | 85 (1) The Year’s Maximum Pensionable Earnings for a year shall be calculated immediately following the date on which the first revision of the Industrial Aggregate in Canada for the month of June of the year preceding that year is published by Statistics Canada.
@ref Reg 85(1) (src:845)
GIVEN `the day on which the first revision of the Industrial Aggregate in Canada for the month of June of the preceding year is published by Statistics Canada` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`reg 85(1) — the first day on which the Year's Maximum Pensionable Earnings for a year is calculated` `the day on which the first revision of the Industrial Aggregate in Canada for the month of June of the preceding year is published by Statistics Canada` MEANS
    `the day on which the first revision of the Industrial Aggregate in Canada for the month of June of the preceding year is published by Statistics Canada` PLUS 1

§§ `Regulation 85(4) and 85(3): the adjusting rules`

-- 85 (4) Where the quotient obtained pursuant to subsection (2) or the product obtained pursuant to paragraph
-- 18(1)(b) or (c) of the Act contains a fraction that is less than one, the fraction shall be expressed as a decimal
-- fraction of three digits after the decimal point and (a) the third digit after the decimal point shall be dropped
-- if that digit is less than five; or (b) the second digit after the decimal point shall be increased by one and the
-- third digit dropped if the third digit is five or greater. A whole number has no fraction and stays as it is.
-- src:850 | (4) Where the quotient obtained pursuant to subsection (2) or the product obtained pursuant to paragraph 18(1)(b) or (c) of the Act contains a fraction that is less than one, the fraction shall be expressed as a decimal fraction of three digits after the decimal point and
-- src:851 | (a) the third digit after the decimal point shall be dropped if that digit is less than five; or
-- src:852 | (b) the second digit after the decimal point shall be increased by one and the third digit dropped if the third digit is five or greater.
@ref Reg 85(4) (src:850-852)
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 85(4) — an amount adjusted to two digits after the decimal point` amount MEANS
    ((FLOOR ((FLOOR (amount TIMES 1000)) DIVIDED BY 10))
     PLUS (IF ((FLOOR (amount TIMES 1000)) MODULO 10) AT LEAST 5 THEN 1 ELSE 0))
    DIVIDED BY 100

-- 85 (3) Where a ratio is calculated pursuant to paragraph 18(1)(b) or (c) of the Act and the ratio contains a
-- fraction that is less than one, the fraction shall be expressed as a decimal fraction of four digits after the
-- decimal point and (a) the fourth digit after the decimal point shall be dropped if that digit is less than five;
-- or (b) the third digit after the decimal point shall be increased by one and the fourth digit dropped if the
-- fourth digit is five or greater.
-- src:847 | (3) Where a ratio is calculated pursuant to paragraph 18(1)(b) or (c) of the Act and the ratio contains a fraction that is less than one, the fraction shall be expressed as a decimal fraction of four digits after the decimal point and
-- src:848 | (a) the fourth digit after the decimal point shall be dropped if that digit is less than five; or
-- src:849 | (b) the third digit after the decimal point shall be increased by one and the fourth digit dropped if the fourth digit is five or greater than five.
@ref Reg 85(3) (src:847-849)
GIVEN ratio IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`reg 85(3) — a ratio adjusted to three digits after the decimal point` ratio MEANS
    ((FLOOR ((FLOOR (ratio TIMES 10000)) DIVIDED BY 10))
     PLUS (IF ((FLOOR (ratio TIMES 10000)) MODULO 10) AT LEAST 5 THEN 1 ELSE 0))
    DIVIDED BY 1000

§§ `Regulation 85(2): the average for a period`

-- 85 (2) Where an average for a period is required pursuant to paragraph 18(1)(b) or (c) of the Act, it shall be
-- calculated as the quotient obtained by dividing the aggregate of the Wage Measure for each month in the period by
-- the number of months in the period, adjusted to two digits after the decimal point in accordance with
-- subsection (4). NOTHING for a period of no months.
-- src:846 | (2) Where an average for a period is required pursuant to paragraph 18(1)(b) or (c) of the Act, it shall be calculated as the quotient obtained by dividing the aggregate of the Wage Measure for each month in the period by the number of months in the period, adjusted to two digits after the decimal point in accordance with subsection (4).
@ref Reg 85(2) (src:846)
GIVEN `the Wage Measure for each month in the period` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`reg 85(2) — the average of the Wage Measure for a period` `the Wage Measure for each month in the period` MEANS
    IF count `the Wage Measure for each month in the period` EQUALS 0
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`reg 85(4) — an amount adjusted to two digits after the decimal point` ((sum `the Wage Measure for each month in the period`) DIVIDED BY (count `the Wage Measure for each month in the period`)))
